<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#aramaic">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aramaic</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aramaic is a Semitic language that became the common language of much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> in the first millennium BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> used it as an administrative language across its provinces, and it continued in use under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> alongside Greek. It later influenced many scripts and remained important in religious texts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aramaic.jpg" alt="Aramaic" width="320" height="91" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathen Payyappilly Palakkappilly (Achayan) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aramaic_written_using_Syriac_letters_(%DC%90%DC%AA%DC%A1%DC%9D%DC%90).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Aramaic is a Northwest Semitic language that originated in the ancient region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, Sinai, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. It belongs to the same group as the Canaanite languages, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and Phoenician, and as Amorite and Ugaritic. Early Aramaic inscriptions date from the 11th century BCE, which places it among the earliest languages to be written down, although its history before the first texts of the 9th century BCE is unknown.</p><p>By around 1000 BCE the Arameans had a string of kingdoms in what is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, Jordan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and the fringes of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Aramaic rose to prominence under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>, whose kings adopted it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> after Arameans had settled in greater numbers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> and in the Assyrian heartland. Written sources of the Neo-Assyrian and Neo-Babylonian empires were bilingual, with Aramaic used beside Akkadian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> continued the practice, and Aramaic gradually became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of most of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, replacing several related <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and his successors ended the Achaemenid order, and by 300 BCE all the main Aramaic-speaking regions were under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, which favoured Greek. During the 3rd century BCE Greek overtook Aramaic in many spheres of public life, especially in the Hellenized cities, but Aramaic went on among the literate classes of native communities and with local authorities.</p><p>Classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, the language of Syriac Christianity, arose in the 1st century CE in Osroene around Edessa. Its golden age was the 4th to 8th centuries, beginning with the translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> known as the Peshitta and including the prose and poetry of Ephrem the Syrian. Missionary activity spread Syriac from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Aramaic is written in the Aramaic alphabet, a descendant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet</a>. Its square style was adopted for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> alphabet, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> alphabet, a cursive form, grew up among Christian communities.</p><p>From the late 7th century Arabic gradually replaced Aramaic as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> after the early Muslim conquests. Aramaic nevertheless remains a spoken, literary and liturgical language. Modern eastern varieties are spoken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>, Mandaeans and some Mizrahi Jews, and Western Aramaic survives in a few villages of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, including Maaloula. Classical varieties are liturgical languages of several West Asian churches.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aramaic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The administrative innovations of Darius I, including the satrapal system, the Royal Road, royal coinage and the use of <b>Aramaic</b> as an administrative language, influenced every subsequent empire of the ancient Near East, including the Seleucid, Parthian and Sasanian states that successively governed the Iranian plateau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Neo-Assyrian practices, including provincial government, relay communications, intelligence networks and the use of <b>Aramaic</b> alongside Akkadian in administration, were inherited by the Neo-Babylonian Empire and then by the Achaemenid Persians, who built consciously on Mesopotamian imperial precedent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A Greek and <b>Aramaic</b> bilingual inscription from Armazi, the epitaph of a noblewoman named Serapit, shows that Aramaic served as a written language before the Georgian alphabet was created.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Arabic replaced Greek, <b>Aramaic</b> and Coptic as the dominant language of the Levant, Mesopotamia and Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal correspondence, preserved chiefly in inscriptions and in later literary citations, shows a chancellery that worked mainly in Greek, alongside <b>Aramaic</b> and local languages, and adapted its documents to the expectations of different recipient communities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The earliest reference to the “House of David” outside the Bible appears on the Tel Dan stele, an <b>Aramaic</b> inscription found in northern Israel in 1993.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
